<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Estanislao López</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1786">1786</a>–<a href="/years/1838">1838</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Estanislao López was governor of Santa Fe in the Argentine Littoral from 1818 until his death and one of the leading Federalist caudillos. An ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Gervasio Artigas</a> in the Federal League, he made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-pilar">Treaty of Pilar</a> in 1820, then fought his former partner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-ramirez">Francisco Ramírez</a>, whose head he displayed in Santa Fe in 1821. In 1831 his men captured the Unitarian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-paz">José María Paz</a>, breaking the Unitarian League.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/estanislao-lopez.jpg" alt="Estanislao López" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Pellegrini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estanislao_L%C3%B3pez.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>López was born in Santa Fe on 26 November 1786, an illegitimate child baptised with his mother's surname at the request of his father, Juan Manuel Roldán. At fifteen he was sent to fight against Malón raids in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-chaco">Gran Chaco</a>. He took part in the retaking of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> from the British invasion of 1806 and fought in the Argentine War of Independence under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-belgrano">Manuel Belgrano</a>, during which he was held prisoner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in 1810.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1816 he led an uprising against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> in which the army of General Juan José Viamonte was forced to surrender. In 1818 he deposed governor Mariano Vera, took the government of Santa Fe and separated the province from the control of Buenos Aires; he was formally elected governor on 1 July 1818 and re-elected indefinitely. He rejected a draft constitution that accepted central rule from Buenos Aires and wrote another, providing for the direct popular election of the governor, which was approved on 26 August 1819.</p><p>Allied with Artigas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-ramirez">Francisco Ramírez</a> of Entre Ríos, he raised large armies against the Supreme Director Juan Martín de Pueyrredón, despite letters from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> urging him and Artigas to cease hostilities and join the national cause. With Ramírez, Carlos María de Alvear and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-miguel-carrera">José Miguel Carrera</a> he defeated José Rondeau at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cepeda-1820">Battle of Cepeda</a> on 1 February 1820, which ended the Supreme Directorship. After Ramírez's death he became the leading figure of the Littoral and on 7 April 1822 signed the Quadrilateral Treaty with Entre Ríos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corrientes">Corrientes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>.</p><p>He sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a> after Juan Lavalle defeated Manuel Dorrego's army at Navarro, and with Rosas beat Lavalle at Puente de Márquez on 26 April 1829, though relations cooled when Rosas made peace with Lavalle without him. On 4 January 1831 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-pact">Federal Pact</a> joined four provinces in a military alliance against the Unitarian League.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>López governed Santa Fe until his death on 15 June 1838 and was succeeded by Domingo Cullen. He is regarded as one of the most influential actors in the Argentine conflicts of the 1820s and 1830s; a stadium and a highway bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Estanislao López” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With Francisco Ramírez and <b>Estanislao López</b> marching to &quot;oust the despots&quot;, Rondeau took 2,000 men to Cepeda and was routed on 1 February 1820.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Artigas's defeat at Tacuarembó in 1820, his allies Francisco Ramírez and <b>Estanislao López</b> made peace with Buenos Aires in the Treaty of Pilar.</blockquote><a href="/state/federal-league">Federal League</a></li><li><blockquote>He was defeated and killed in July 1821, and <b>Estanislao López</b> displayed his head in Santa Fe.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-entre-rios">Republic of Entre Ríos</a></li><li><blockquote>In May 1831 Paz was captured when soldiers of <b>Estanislao López</b> brought down his horse with boleadoras.</blockquote><a href="/state/unitarian-league">Unitarian League</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-argentina">Santa Fe</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. Died in the Argentine Confederation. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-31.63,-60.7&amp;year=1819" aria-label="Santa Fe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Estanislao López"><image href="/maps/globes/g300s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4949 5140h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4949 5140h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-31.63,-60.7&amp;year=1819">What was here in 1819? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
